It&#x27;s very easy to accidentally write buffer overflows, use-after-frees or other memory-safety bugs in C. Modern tooling can probably catch some of these better than in the days of yore but the fundamentals of the language are still the same.<p>If e.g. the standard array type in a language has built-in bounds checks that error out when you try to write to an index that&#x27;s outside of the array, you can&#x27;t trivially create buffer overflows simply by writing to the wrong index or by trying to write n + 10 bytes into an n-byte array.<p>But C in itself has no guardrails against something like<p><pre><code>  char buffer[100];
  strcpy(buffer, some_user_supplied_string_with_200_chars);
</code></pre>
Of course most memory safety bugs aren&#x27;t trivial like that since C programmers have hopefully learned to avoid the simple mistakes. But there can be more complicated and difficult to spot cases, and C has no guardrails against those either.
